# dbt build output
target/
target-sample/
dbt_packages/
logs/
.user.yml

# Credentials. A real project's profiles.yml lives in ~/.dbt/ and is never committed.
profiles.yml
.env
*.pem
*.p8

# ...with one deliberate exception, named by its exact path. The worked example's
# profiles.yml IS committed: every value in it is either a local file path or an
# env_var() reference, and it is the file that documents how to point the same project at
# DuckDB, BigQuery, or Snowflake. Read its header before copying the pattern.
#
# The path is written out in full rather than as `**/dbt_project/profiles.yml` on purpose.
# A glob would silently un-ignore the profiles.yml of every use-case added later, removing
# the backstop exactly where a real credential is most likely to be typed. A new use case
# that genuinely needs its profile committed adds its own line here, which is a reviewable
# diff.
#
# Keep this path in step with where the example actually lives. It moved into the pack, and
# the stale `use-cases/...` form matched nothing — which silently un-committed the file and
# left a fresh clone unable to run the example at all.
!skill-packs/dbt-skills/use-cases/example-order-revenue-mart/dbt_project/profiles.yml

# DuckDB databases created by the worked example
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# `.venv*/` rather than naming each one: a per-use-case env like `.venv-dbt/` is the normal
# way to pin a dbt adapter, and the bare `.venv/` form silently lets 5k vendored files be
# staged. Covers `.venv/` and `.venv-dbt/`, both previously listed by hand.
.venv*/
venv/
.pytest_cache/


# OS
.DS_Store

# NOTE: package-lock.yml SHOULD be committed, so CI resolves the same package versions
#       you did.
# NOTE: the synthetic artifacts under use-cases/example-order-revenue-mart/artifacts/ are
#       committed on purpose — they are the worked example's fixtures, not build output,
#       and are excluded from the target/ rule by their path.
!**/artifacts/target/
!**/artifacts/target/*.json

# Graphify
graphify-out/
# graphify-out/cost.json      # local only
# graphify-out/cache/         # optional: commit for speed, skip to keep repo small

# Local agentmemory artifacts for this scaffold
.claude/agentmemory.json
.claude/graph-state.json
.claude/checkpoints/
# Rust TOON serializer build output (scripts/build_toon_rs.sh)
rust/toon/bin/

# skill-map project scope. The DB and caches stay untracked, but
# settings.json IS committed: it carries the team-shared analyzer policy
# (core/reference-broken's ignored-references match-list -- the sanctioned
# escape hatch for targets that are known-dead by design: generated trees,
# consumer-workspace futures, naming-convention examples). sm's own
# .skill-map/.gitignore keeps the generated artifacts out; this carve-out
# only lets the committed policy file through. The DB and caches remain local
# state, which is also what keeps the CI activation-drift gate seeing a clean
# tree after a scan runs (a scan never rewrites settings.json).
.skill-map/*
!.skill-map/settings.json
.continue/

# Local runtime state stores (machine-specific generated binaries)
data/state_store.db/
data/stream_store/